-# Live
+# hex-voice
 
-An Expo SDK 57 voice app with GPT-Live 1, web search, a weather tool, and six [AI Elements Persona](https://elements.ai-sdk.dev/components/persona) variants. You can speak during replies, choose a voice, and keep an iPhone conversation running in the background.
+An iPhone app for calling [hex](https://github.com/AVGVSTVS96/hex), your personal assistant, by voice. You talk with GPT-Live 1; hex's Claude Code session does the real work behind it and tells the voice what to say. Calls keep running with the screen locked, and your orb stays on the Lock Screen and in the Dynamic Island so one tap brings you back. You can talk over replies, pick any GPT-Live voice, and pull the orb down to read the conversation as it happens.
 
-https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/dd22258b-18b2-48fb-9d93-4a928df576cc
+Built on David Mokos's [expo-gpt-live](https://github.com/davidmokos/expo-gpt-live) template (MIT): Expo SDK 57, native Expo UI controls, and six [AI Elements Persona](https://elements.ai-sdk.dev/components/persona) animations.
+
+```
+iPhone ── WebRTC offer ──► hex voice plugin ──► gpt-live-1 session
+   ▲                        (over Tailscale)       │ hands requests to
+   └──────── audio, captions ◄─────────────────────┘ hex's Claude Code
+```
+
+The app has no server or OpenAI key of its own. hex's [voice plugin](https://github.com/AVGVSTVS96/claude-plugins-official/tree/main/external_plugins/voice) starts each session with hex's instructions, so the app, hex's call page and its phone number all reach the same hex.
 
 ## Get started
 
-You need Node.js 22.13+, macOS with Xcode 26.4+, and an OpenAI API key with access to GPT-Live 1.
+You need hex running with its voice plugin, served to your tailnet (`tailscale serve --bg --https=8790 http://127.0.0.1:8790`), Tailscale on the iPhone, Node.js 22.13+, and macOS with Xcode 26.4+.
 
 ```sh
-git clone https://github.com/davidmokos/expo-gpt-live.git
-cd expo-gpt-live
+git clone https://github.com/AVGVSTVS96/hex-voice.git
+cd hex-voice
 npm ci
-npm run setup
+cp .env.example .env.local   # set EXPO_PUBLIC_HEX_URL to your server's tailnet address
 ```
 
-Add your key to `OPENAI_API_KEY` in `.env.local`. Setup creates the app's access token and local server address. It preserves an existing `.env.local`.
-
-Set `ios.bundleIdentifier` in `app.json` to your own identifier, such as `com.yourname.live`. Keep your Mac and iPhone on the same Wi-Fi. Connect and unlock the phone, trust the Mac, and enable Developer Mode in iPhone settings. Then run:
+Set `ios.bundleIdentifier` in `app.json` to your own, connect and unlock the phone, enable Developer Mode, and run:
 
 ```sh
 npm run ios:device
 ```
 
-Expo handles signing, installation, and the development server. For later JavaScript changes, run `npm start`. Use `npm run ios` for a simulator. Expo Go cannot run this app because WebRTC and Rive require a native build.
-
-For a browser, run `npm run web` and enter the `API_TOKEN` value from `.env.local` when you tap Start. Use `npm run android` for Android; background calls are implemented on iOS only.
-
-## Environment
-
-`.env.local` is ignored by Git.
-
-| Variable                | Used by                                                                     |
-| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
-| `OPENAI_API_KEY`        | Server only. Your OpenAI key.                                               |
-| `API_TOKEN`             | Server. Generated by setup.                                                 |
-| `EXPO_PUBLIC_API_TOKEN` | Native app. Must match `API_TOKEN`.                                         |
-| `EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL`   | Native app. Your Mac's reachable address on port 8088, or a hosted API URL. |
-
-Never put the OpenAI key in an `EXPO_PUBLIC_` variable. The app's shared token is for local development and private builds. Add user authentication before making the backend public.
-
-For an app that works without your Mac, follow [EAS deployment](docs/deploy.md). The template has no linked EAS project.
+Expo handles signing and installation. Expo Go cannot run this app because WebRTC and Rive need a native build.
 
 ## Development
 
 ```sh
 npm run check
-npm run format
 ```
 
-The screen is in `src/screens/live-screen.tsx`. Native controls and Persona renderers are in `src/components`. Call lifecycle and transports are in `src/live`; session configuration and tools are in `src/server`. See [tools](docs/tools.md) to add your own.
-
-Voices are fixed for each call. Persona choices can change at any time. Both choices are saved locally. Calls end after ten minutes; this does not enforce an account spending limit.
-
-See [dependency notes](docs/dependencies.md) for the Xcode workaround and an upstream advisory.
+The screen is in `src/screens/live-screen.tsx`, native controls and Persona renderers in `src/components`, and the call lifecycle in `src/live`, along with the call's Live Activity (`call-activity.tsx`, built with `expo-widgets`). Its orb stills are rendered from the Persona files by `npm run activity-orbs`. See [dependency notes](docs/dependencies.md) for the Xcode workaround.
 
-MIT. Persona assets retain their [upstream license and attribution](src/components/persona/assets/README.md).
+MIT. Persona assets keep their [upstream license and attribution](src/components/persona/assets/README.md).
